A host keeps the extensions jiti compiled in the runtime folder.

pi's extension loader gave jiti no fsCache, so jiti cached compiled
extensions in os.tmpdir()/jiti. A host's child has no TMPDIR, so that
was /tmp/jiti, which a sweep of /tmp empties: on 21 November one
emptied it mid-load, a conversation's reload failed with ENOENT on a
compiled file there, and nine of its tools were gone until a later
reload. The account's runtime folder is private to it and no sweep
of /tmp reaches it, so the cache goes to $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pi-jiti. A
pi without a runtime folder keeps jiti's default, as before. On tmpfs
the cache is memory, one copy for the account (about 51 MB warm for
three packages, measured on another machine), not one per child.

Apply only to the host's private pi build; ordinary interactive pi stays
unchanged.

--- a/packages/coding-agent/src/core/extensions/loader.ts
+++ b/packages/coding-agent/src/core/extensions/loader.ts
@@ -56,6 +56,18 @@ function getCreateJiti(): Promise<typeof createJiti> {
 	return createJitiPromise;
 }
 
+/**
+ * Where jiti keeps the extensions it compiled: the account's runtime folder
+ * when it has one, which is private to the account and no sweep of /tmp
+ * empties, else jiti's own default, os.tmpdir()/jiti. A host's child has no
+ * TMPDIR, so that default was /tmp/jiti, and a sweep of /tmp mid-load failed
+ * the load and dropped its extension's tools until the next reload.
+ */
+function jitiCacheDir(): string | true {
+	const runtime = process.env.XDG_RUNTIME_DIR;
+	return runtime ? path.join(runtime, "pi-jiti") : true;
+}
+
 let virtualModulesPromise: Promise<Record<string, unknown>> | undefined;
 
 function getVirtualModules(): Promise<Record<string, unknown>> {
@@ -710,6 +722,7 @@ async function loadExtensionModule(extensionPath: string, cacheToken?: Extension
 			: { alias: getAliases() };
 	const jiti = createJitiImpl(import.meta.url, {
 		moduleCache: false,
+		fsCache: jitiCacheDir(),
 		...resolutionOptions,
 	});
 
